  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a4da58d2dc2409c459ef20449bf92a90, type: 3}
  m_Name: Shear
  m_EditorClassIdentifier:
  lessonId: civil-craft-shear-lesson-v1
  lessonTitle: Shear
  lessonImage: {fileID: 0}
  popupDescription: "<b><#E09500>Shear</color></b> is an internal force that acts across a section of a structural member and resists the tendency of adjacent parts to slide relative to each other.\n\nKey points:\n- Shear forces develop when loads act on a structure.\n- Shear is different from bending, although both can occur in the same member.\n- In a simply supported beam, shear is commonly significant near the supports.\n- Excessive shear can cause cracking or structural failure.\n\nThis lesson has been added to your <b><#E09500>Almanac</color></b>."
  almanacImage: {fileID: 0}
  lessonDescription: "<b><#E09500>Shear</color></b> is an internal force that acts across a section of a structural member and <b><#E09500>resists adjacent parts sliding relative to each other</color></b>. Shear forces develop when loads act on a structure. When a heavy vehicle crosses a bridge beam, its weight acts downward while the supports provide upward reactions.\n\n<b><#E09500>Shear and bending are different internal effects</color></b>, but both can occur in the same structural member. In a simply supported beam carrying a load near the middle, shear force is often greatest in the <b><#E09500>regions near the supports</color></b>. Its significance depends on the load and how the bridge is supported.\n\nA structural member must safely withstand the shear forces acting on it. <b><#E09500>Excessive shear</color></b> can cause cracking or structural failure. Review the members near the supports as well as the safety of the entire bridge.\n\nRonan's southern connection must carry a heavy vehicle across a 100-meter gap with different road elevations. During testing, observe how the structural members near the supports respond as the vehicle enters, moves along the span, and approaches the opposite side. Continue observing until the vehicle has completed the crossing."
